        "relativePath": "SKILL.md",
        "content": "# LISA clustering analysis\n\nUse LISA (Local Indicators of Spatial Association, e.g. Local Moran's I) to identify local clusters and spatial outliers for a variable.\n\n## When to use\n\nUse this skill when the user wants to find spatial clusters, hot spots / cold spots, or spatial outliers for a numeric variable across geographic features.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. If spatial weights are required and not already available, create them first using `executeApi` with `commandId: \"geoda.spatial-weights\"` and `input: { datasetName, type, k?, orderOfContiguity?, ... }` (queen or knn). The returned `weightsId` is needed for the LISA step.\n2. Run the LISA analysis using `executeApi` with `commandId: \"geoda.lisa\"` and `input: { datasetName, variableName, method, weightsId, permutation?, significanceThreshold?, k?, quantile? }`. `method` is one of: localMoran, localGeary, localG, localGStar, quantileLisa.\n3. Save the LISA result as a new dataset and visualize it on the map using `executeApi` with `commandId: \"map.add-layer\"` and `input: { datasetName, layerType, colorBy, colorType: \"unique\", colorMap }` using a qualitative color scheme for the cluster labels.\n4. Explain the results in context: what the clusters mean, where the hot/cold spots are, and what the spatial outliers indicate.\n\n## Rules\n\n- STRICT RULE: Never use datasets generated from previous LISA tools (dataset name with a \"lisa_\" prefix) as input for a new LISA analysis.\n- For clustering analysis, always perform a spatial statistical test (e.g. Local Moran's I) rather than relying on visual inspection.\n- If the input is a road or line dataset, buffer it by 1 meter first (using `executeApi` with `commandId: \"geo.spatial-query\"`), save the buffered road as a new dataset, and use that for the LISA analysis.\n- When a new dataset has been generated by any tool, save it as a new dataset in kepler.gl first, then use that new dataset for the spatial analysis."
